  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nClouds are expected to clear behind the cold front this\nafternoon, though middle/high clouds may encroach in the \nevening with downstream advection of Midwest moisture. This \nshould mostly limit frost chances in southeast OH, northern West\nVirginia, and southwest Pennsylvania, keeping temperatures in \nthe 40s. For northwest Pennsylvania and eastern Ohio, there is \nhigh confidence in clearing after midnight, which may lend to a \nperiod of efficient radiative cooling. The question is, how \ncool will temperatures drop. HREF has a 30% to 70% chance of \nless than 37F, and a less than a 20% chance of a freeze. This \nmay lend to mostly terrain dependent frost formation. Freezes \nmay be possible ina few insulated, small drainage basins.\n\nUnlike tonight, Monday night into Tuesday morning has a high\nconfidence in clear skies and calm winds. NBM generally has \ntemperatures less than 37F >50% likely north of I-70, and a 30%\nto 60% chance of a freeze north of US-422. This may very well be\nthe last frost/freeze of the season with the rest of the CPC May\noutlooks favoring warm and no other frosty mornings in the\ncurrent forecast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nIsolated showers may continue this morning with lingering\nelevated instability behind overnight convection, but coverage\nis expected to decrease towards daybreak with the loss of\ninstability. Coverage will then increase south of I-80 this \nmorning as the actual cold front makes its official passage. \nConvergence ahead of the front along with perhaps a weak low- \nshortwave will develop some showers in the 8am to 4pm hours, \ndeparting from northwest to southeast with the front. Clearing \nis expected thereafter. Moist and warm air aloft is expected to \nmostly limit thunder chances, but an isolated storm or two could\nnot be ruled out south of I-70 with some instability generation\npossible before the rains departure. Even the high-end totals \nare below 0.25\" of additional accumulation.\n\nHigh pressure and dry conditions return Sunday night through\nTuesday. Another longwave trough is expected to dive across the\narea Tuesday night through Wednesday. LREF clustering shows\nthere is uncertainty with the depth (or strength) of the\nsecondary trough. Solutions with a stronger trough could bring\nas much as 1\" to 1.5\" across the area, while weaker solutions\nmay only bring a couple tenths of an inch. Nonetheless, there is\nuncertainty affiliated with this system. Most of this does not\nlead to any flooding concerns.\n\nThere will also be some Wednesday afternoon convective nuance.\nIn one scenario with higher rain chances and cloud coverage \nwith the warm front, temperatures likely won't be able to \nrecover and garner instability before the cold front, resulting \nin lower convective chances. On the other hand, if rain and \nclouds briefly depart in a narrow warm sector Wednesday, \nafternoon severe weather could not be ruled out completely with\nsteep lapse rates and shear, but this still remains quite low \nconfidence.",
